
// ngplc -- the NGPL compiler, attempt 2.
//
// Written in the bootstrap subset of NGPL, and compiled by itself.  It
// compiles the core-2 subset described in DESIGN.md to static Linux ELF
// executables that speak only to the kernel, for six targets: x86-64,
// i386, aarch64, arm, riscv64 and riscv32.
//
// The compiler is twenty-five source files, read as if they were one
// and named in order by build/sources.sh and by the @build recipe in
// main.ngpl.  The order is part of the program: an enum and a unit must
// be declared before they are used.  Build it with
//
//     ngplc --build src/main.ngpl
//
// or, for the first stage, through the bootstrap interpreter, which
// takes the same list:
//
//     python -m interp <sources…> -- <sources…> -o build/ngplc
//
// The pipeline is a chain of pure functions over flat arrays:
//     bytes -> lex -> Toks -> parse -> Ast -> check -> lower -> Ir
//           -> emit -> code bytes -> elf -> a.out (or the -o name)
//
// Control flow policy (CLAUDE.md): decisions are data.  The lexer runs
// on a byte-class table and two glyph hashes rather than a branch
// tree; the code generator materializes comparisons (setcc), selects
// with conditional moves where both sides are safe to read, and turns
// dense dispatch into jump tables.  Branches that remain are cold
// paths (aborts) or semantically required (short-circuit with an
// effectful right side).
//
// This file holds the token kinds and the helpers over raw byte runs;
// what follows it is listed in build/sources.sh.

// ===========================================================================
// Token kinds
// ===========================================================================

